<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Otto the Illustrious</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 851–912</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Otto I, Duke of Saxony · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottonian-dynasty">Ottonian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Otto the Illustrious was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> count of the Liudolfing family who held a dominant position in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> from 880 until his death in 912. A son of Count Liudolf, he succeeded his brother Bruno, who was killed fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a>, and he is counted as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-saxony">Duke of Saxony</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a> was elected king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a> in 919, beginning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottonian-dynasty">Ottonian dynasty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/otto-the-illustrious.jpg" alt="Otto the Illustrious" width="300" height="300" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Otto_I,_Duke_of_Saxony.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Otto was the younger son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> count Liudolf, who died in 866 and was the progenitor of the dynasty, and of Oda of Gandersheim, daughter of the Saxon princeps Billung. His eldest brother Bruno was heir to their father's estates. His sister Liutgard became queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a> in 876 as consort of the Carolingian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-younger">Louis the Younger</a>, a marriage that expressed Liudolf's dominant position in the Saxon lands.</p><p>Around 873 Otto married Hathui, probably a daughter of the Frankish princeps militiae Henry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a>, of the Popponid house. They had two sons, Thankmar and Liudolf, who died before him, and a third son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a>. Their daughter Oda married the Carolingian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zwentibold">Zwentibold of Lotharingia</a>, son of Emperor Arnulf.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>A charter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-younger">Louis the Younger</a> of 26 January 877 described South Thuringia as lying in Otto's county. Otto succeeded Bruno after Bruno fell fighting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> invaders at the Battle of Lüneburg Heath on 2 February 880. He held vast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> and Thuringian estates, and later sources call him dux, while a charter of 28 January 897 describes him as marchio and places <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eichsfeld">Eichsfeld</a> within his march.</p><p>In 908 he was lay abbot of Hersfeld Abbey. Although related to Carolingian kings, he had only loose ties to their court and rarely left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>. He remained a regional East Frankish prince on good terms with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-younger">Louis the Younger</a> and Emperor Arnulf, who rarely interfered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> autonomy. He also made himself overlord of neighbouring Slavic tribes in the east, such as the Daleminzi.</p><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/widukind-of-corvey">Widukind of Corvey</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> and Franconian people offered Otto the kingship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a> after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-child">Louis the Child</a> in 911, the last Carolingian king there. Otto declined, possibly because of his age, and suggested Duke Conrad of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a> instead. The report is considered doubtful.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Otto died on 30 November 912 at the Pfalz of Wallhausen and was buried in the church of Gandersheim Abbey. His family came to be called the Liudolfinger after his father, and after the accession of his grandson, Emperor Otto the Great, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottonian-dynasty">Ottonian dynasty</a>. The epithet the Illustrious was given to him by later authors.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Otto the Illustrious” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son Bruno led the Saxons against the Vikings and was killed fighting them in 880, and his younger son <b>Otto the Illustrious</b> held a dominant position in Saxony and Thuringia until his death in 912.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
